  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 130 AM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nA stationary mid-level low trapped underneath this ridge block \npattern near the Bahamas is expected to finally eject further out \ninto the Atlantic by the Wednesday-Thursday time frame, along with \nan upper level ridge expected to rebuild back over the region from \nthe Southwestern Gulf and Caribbean. Additionally, several shortwave \ntroughs are expected to advect across the mountain west and towards \nthe central and eastern U.S., which will help to flatten the high \npressure currently in place and allow winds to become less breezy by \nThursday and Friday. Therefore, while scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are still expected each day through the end of the \nweek, the weather as a whole should be much less unsettled than it \ncurrently is right now. Low level wind flow would be expected to \nshift back to a NE/ENE direction and favor inland South Florida for \nthe highest rain chances. With the upper level ridge helping \nsuppress deep convection from Thursday and into the weekend, QPF \neach day is expected for less than one inch, which results in no \nconcerns currently for excessive rainfall through the period. High-\nend scenarios and clusters hint at potential for perhaps up to 2-4 \ninches of rain in a two day period, which further does not support \nevidence for flooding risk at this current time.\n\nAs a result of the information above, the concern to monitor into \nthe end of the week is the ongoing King Tide cycle contributing to \nexacerbation of flooding with any rainfall occurring. Given that \nsome level of showers and embedded storms are likely each day, the \nKing Tide cycles can exacerbate flooding in the vulnerable coastal \nlocations. While the peak tides will be during the beginning to \nmiddle of the week, moderate coastal flooding may persist through \nthe end of the week as we come out of this full moon phase.\n\nHigh temperatures will begin to rebound by a few degrees for the \nlong term period, with highs rising back to the mid 80s for \nSE Florida and  upper 80s for SW Florida.",
